4.text:
With the usual Marshallian convention the competitive price is the unique
positive root of

\begin{equation*}
P_{S}(Q)=P_{D}(Q)\iff 
\ln \!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)=18\,e^{-\arctan Q}\;,
\qquad Q^{3}>2 ,
\end{equation*}

$P_{S}$ increasing and $P_{D}$ decreasing.  In rad

\begin{equation*}
f(Q)=\ln (Q^{3}-2)-18e^{-\arctan Q}=0
\end{equation*}

gives

* $Q^{*}\simeq 4.68\!-\!4.70$,
* $Q^{*}\approx 4.69$,

and

\begin{equation*}
P^{*}= \ln\!\left({Q^{*}}^{3}-2\right)
     =18e^{-\arctan Q^{*}}\simeq 4.61\!-\!4.63 ,
\end{equation*}

$P^{*}\approx4.62$ in the units of $P$, $P^{*}Q^{*}\approx 21.6\!-\!21.7$.
Put $q_{0}=\sqrt[3]{3}$ so that $P_{S}(q_{0})=0$, $q_{0}\simeq1.44$.
With $t=\arctan q$,

\begin{equation*}
\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}P_{D}(q)dq
   =18\int_{0}^{u^{*}}e^{-t}\sec^{2}t\,dt,
\qquad u^{*}=\arctan Q^{*}\simeq1.361 ,
\end{equation*}

and with the usual Simpson/trapezoidal quadrature

\begin{equation*}
I_{D}(Q^{*})=\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}18e^{-\arctan q}dq\approx
32.4\!-\!32.6\approx 32.5 ,
\end{equation*}

the gross value of demand up to the equilibrium.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
CS^{*}=I_{D}(Q^{*})-P^{*}Q^{*}
      \approx 32.5-21.66\approx 10.8\!-\!11.0 ,
\end{equation*}

about $10.8$ monetary units of consumer surplus.

\begin{equation*}
I_{S}(Q^{*})=\int_{q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}\!\ln\!\left(q^{3}-2\right)dq
        \approx 9.8\!-\!10.0\approx 9.9 ,
\end{equation*}

so that on the textbook $PS_{0}=P^{*}Q^{*}-\int_{q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}P_{S}$
convention

\begin{equation*}
PS_{0}^{*}=P^{*}Q^{*}-I_{S}(Q^{*})\approx 21.66-9.89\approx 11.7\!-\!12.0
\end{equation*}

and the conventional consumer-plus-producer surplus

\begin{equation*}
W_{0}^{*}=CS^{*}+PS_{0}^{*}
       =I_{D}(Q^{*})-I_{S}(Q^{*})
       =\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}P_{D}(q)dq-\int_{q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}P_{S}(q)dq
\end{equation*}

is

\begin{equation*}
W_{0}^{*}\approx 32.5-9.9\approx 22\!-\!23 ,
\qquad W_{0}^{*}\simeq 22.6 ,
\end{equation*}

$CS^{*}\simeq10.8$, $PS_{0}^{*}\simeq11.8$ at $Q^{*}\simeq4.69$,
$P^{*}\simeq4.62$.  This is the net area $\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}
\big[P_{D}(q)-P_{S}(q)\big]dq$ between demand $18e^{-\arctan q}$ and the
inverse supply $\ln(q^{3}-2)$ up to the competitive quantity.

In the strictly economic producer-surplus form $PS^{*}=\int_{q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}
\big[P^{*}-P_{S}(q)\big]dq=P^{*}(Q^{*}-q_{0})-I_{S}$ one obtains
$PS^{*}\approx P^{*}(Q^{*}-q_{0})-I_{S}\approx5.1$, $CS^{*}\approx10.8$
and $CS^{*}+PS^{*}=I_{D}-P^{*}q_{0}-I_{S}\approx15.9\!-\!16.0$,
the $P^{*}q_{0}\approx6.66$ smaller figure normally quoted as the
$W_{D}-W_{S}$ area with the $P_{S}\equiv0$ truncation for $q<q_{0}$.
The $Q^{*}\approx4.69$, $P^{*}\approx4.62$ equilibrium and the
$\,I_{D}\approx32.5$, $I_{S}\approx9.9$ integrals are stable across the
numerical estimates. ∎

